  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 210 PM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Cloudy and cool conditions and patches of \ndrizzle/mist continue through the day today.\n\nYesterday's cold front looks to have stalled along the coast and \ninto South Carolina, allowing the CAD wedge airmass to set up over \ncentral NC. That has led to the cool, cloudy, and drizzly/misty \nconditions that have been ongoing. This should continue through the \nday today, with highs looking to only reach the mid 60s to \npotentially around 70 in the south. Much of the light rain should be \ndone for the afternoon, with some light drizzle/mist continuing in \nthe northeastern portions of the CWA. Late tonight, another round of \nlight rain may be possible in the far southeast as another wave \nmoves along the stalled front. \n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Temperatures will moderate through the week, with \nrain likely returning by the end of next weekend.\n\nAs the cold front along the Atlantic shoreline slides offshore \nMonday with high pressure building into the Ohio Valley, cloud cover \nwill be slow to depart central North Carolina. Considering there \nwill be more sun in the west Monday afternoon than the east, this \nwill likely be a rare case where the Triad has the warmest \ntemperatures, although all locations are likely to reach the mid \n70s. Northeasterly flow will bring slightly cooler highs on Tuesday, \nbut all locations are expected to warm into the 80s by Thursday.\n\nThursday should be the warmest day out of the next seven, with cloud \ncover beginning to increase by Friday in advance of a storm that \nwill move north from the Gulf into the Mississippi River Valley. The \ncurrent forecast introduces a chance of rain beginning Saturday \nnight and continues to increase into Sunday. There are still many \ndays for the specifics to change with this part of the forecast.",
